  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Giant stone heads lie face-up on a Turkish mountain. Over two thousand metres up Nemrut, broken off statues that once stood nine metres tall.",
        "visual_query": "stone head statue mountain summit",
        "visual_fallback": "stone statue"
      },
      {
        "narration": "King Antiochus of Commagene built this tomb-sanctuary in 62 BC: himself, two lions, two eagles, and gods who were half Greek, half Persian.",
        "visual_query": "anatolian mountain ancient terrace ruins",
        "visual_fallback": "mountain ruins"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Behind the terraces rises a cone of loose rubble, forty-nine metres high. And the king's grave? Still missing.",
        "visual_query": "rocky mountain summit gravel cone",
        "visual_fallback": "rocky summit"
      },
      {
        "narration": "That rubble is why. Dig into loose rock and the hole pours shut behind you, and archaeologists think that was the entire design.",
        "visual_query": "loose scree rock slope mountain",
        "visual_fallback": "loose scree"
      },
      {
        "narration": "A German engineer, Karl Sester, excavated here in 1881. Nearly a century and a half later, Antiochus is still somewhere inside his own mountain.",
        "visual_query": "eastern turkey mountain sunrise ruins",
        "visual_fallback": "mountain sunrise"
      }
    ],
